  volume    =  34,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "135--154",
  abstract  = "In Egypt, state formation occurred much more rapidly after the
               adoption of farming than in many other parts of the ancient Near
               East. Furthermore, the Egyptian state lasted longer and was more
               stable than most Empires established elsewhere. This paper argues
               that successful states in the ancient world depended on the
               ability of elites to extract a surplus from farmers and other
               producers. This ability was greatest when the population was
               immobile. The success of the Pharaohs was due to the geography of
