  volume    =  106,
  number    =  4,
  pages     = "720--741",
  abstract  = "Are citizens competent to assess the performance of incumbent
               politicians? Observational studies cast doubt on voter competence
               by documenting several biases in retrospective assessments of
               performance. However, these studies are open to alternative
               interpretations because of the complexity of the real world. In
               this article, we show that these biases in retrospective
               evaluations occur even in the simplified setting of experimental
               games. In three experiments, our participants (1) overweighted
               recent relative to overall incumbent performance when made aware
               of an election closer rather than more distant from that event,
