  publisher = "Cambridge University Press (CUP)",
  volume    =  101,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "273--288",
  abstract  = "Elected officials are commonly accused of beingideologically
               rigid, or failing to alter their positions in response to
               relevant policy information. We examine this phenomenon with a
               theory in which politicians have private information about their
               ideological leanings and expected policy consequences. The theory
