               foreign powers, and the Indian Wars in the last half of the
               nineteenth century. However, the bulk of homesteading actually
               took place in the early twentieth century, long after these
               threats to federal ownership ceased. We argue that this “late
               homesteading” was also an effort to enforce federal rights, but
               in response to a different threat—a legal one. Questionable
               federal land policies in the late nineteenth century dispossessed
               massive amounts of Indigenous lands, and exposed the federal
               government to legal, rather than violent, conflict. Late
               homesteading was used to make the dispossession permanent, even
