  publisher = "Cambridge University Press (CUP)",
  volume    =  6,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "309--333",
  abstract  = "AbstractFrom 1959 to 1964, Prince Edward County, Virginia, dodged
               a court desegregation order by refusing to operate public
               schools. Though the county played an integral role in the
               national battle over civil rights, scholars and journalists have
               largely neglected Prince Edward's role in the national drama of
               race. In 1951, Black high school students went on strike to
               protest unequal school facilities. This strike led to an NAACP
               lawsuit that became one of five decided inBrown v. Board of
