               Most White students attended a private, segregated academy; over
               three-quarters of Black Prince Edward students lost some or all
               of those years of education. White county leaders believed they
               were creating a blueprint for defying desegregation in the rural
               South and perhaps, they hoped, throughout much of the United
               States. Using archival materials, interviews and secondary
