               to assess the effects of divided government on support for the
               president's preferred legislation once it reaches the House
               floor. By disaggregating analysis into specific policy areas, the
               results demonstrate that the impact of divided government on
               presidential support varies systematically across policy areas by
               the degree to which the policy cross-cuts society. Broadly
               cross-cutting policy areas are more prone to partisan conflict
               and, hence, to the effects of divided government, while more
               sector-specific policy areas generate conflict along bipartisan
               and institutional lines as well as along partisan ones. Support
               scores on sector specific policies, as a result, are not
               significantly prone to the influence of divided government. The
