  volume    =  129,
  number    =  1,
  pages     = "379--433",
  abstract  = "AbstractThe Voting Rights Act of 1965, called one of the most
               effective pieces of civil rights legislation in U.S. history,
               generated dramatic increases in black voter registration across
               the South. We ask whether the increase in black voting rights was
               accompanied by an increase in blacks’ share of public spending.
               We exploit a key provision of the act—removal of literacy tests
               at registration—for identification. Employing a triple-difference
               framework over a 20-year period, we find that counties with
               higher black population shares in former literacy test states saw
               greater increases in both voter turnout and state transfers than
