  publisher = "Wiley",
  volume    =  47,
  number    =  3,
  pages     = "503--522",
  abstract  = "Recent years have witnessed many efforts to understand
               legislative productivity and gridlock. However, despite
               theoretical and empirical contributions to how preferences and
               institutions shape political gridlock's level (e.g., Krehbiel
               1996, 1998) and empirical evidence about how parties may affect
               political gridlock (e.g., Binder 1999; Coleman 1999), we lack a
               comprehensive perspective theoretically and empirically examining
               preferences, institutions, and parties. We overcome this
               deficiency by modeling conditions for gridlock as a function of
