               we expect to see forming? I examine classic cooperative and new
               noncooperative game theoretic approaches to answering these
               questions. Classic approaches have focused especially on the
               first question and have produced powerful results. However, these
               approaches suffer from a number of weaknesses. New work attempts
               to address these shortcomings by modeling coalition formation as
               an explicitly noncooperative process. This new research
               reintroduces the problem of coalitional instability
               characteristic of cooperative approaches, but in a dynamic
               setting. Although in some settings, classic solutions are
               recovered, in others this new work shows that outcomes are highly
               sensitive, not only to bargaining protocols, but also to the
               forms of commitment that can be externally enforced. This point
