  author    = "Wowak, Adam J and Busenbark, John R and Hambrick, Donald C",
  journal   = "Adm. Sci. Q.",
  publisher = "SAGE Publications",
  volume    =  67,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "553--593",
  abstract  = "Business leaders have traditionally avoided wading into society’s
               debates. Yet more and more CEOs are taking visible public stands
               on hotly contested issues, engaging in what has come to be called
               CEO sociopolitical activism. Despite its growing prevalence and
               potentially major implications, this class of executive behaviors
               remains largely unexplored by organizational scholars. Our study
               tests and elaborates on stakeholder alignment theory to
               investigate the influence of CEO activism on employees’ attitudes
               and behaviors, particularly its effects on employees’
               organizational commitment and support for the ideology
               underpinning the CEO’s public stance. Our theoretical predictions
               hinge on the degree of alignment between the CEO’s stance and the
               prevailing ideological tilt of the employee population, as well
               as the degree to which employees view the CEO as a credible
               leader. We test our ideas in the context of a highly publicized
               letter signed by nearly 100 public company CEOs in opposition to
