  volume    =  93,
  number    =  2,
  pages     = "299--309",
  abstract  = "A key premise of partisan theories of congressional organization
               is that majority status confers substantial procedural
               advantages. In this article, we take advantage of changes in
               party control of the House and Senate, such as that following the
               Republicans' historic victory in the midterm elections of 1994,
               to assess the value of majority status in terms of contributions
               from access-seeking political action committees (PACs). We
