  volume    =  90,
  number    =  6,
  pages     = "2685--2721",
  abstract  = "This paper studies the role of private sector companies in the
               development of local amenities. We use evidence from one of the
               largest multinationals of the 20th century: the United Fruit
               Company (UFCo). The firm was given a large land concession in
               Costa Rica—one of the so‐called “Banana Republics”—from 1899 to
               1984. Using administrative census data with census‐block
               geo‐references from 1973 to 2011, we implement a geographic
               regression discontinuity design that exploits a land assignment
               that is orthogonal to our outcomes of interest. We find that the
               firm had a positive and persistent effect on living standards.
               Company documents explain that a key concern at the time was to
