  address   = "Cheltenham, England",
  pages     = "73--87",
  abstract  = "This chapter examines how Latin America's intellectual property
               (IP) landscape has evolved under the influence of the WTO's TRIPS
               Agreement, a growing network of FTAs, and the resultant
               ‘TRIPS-plus’ provisions. Examples like the United
               States–Mexico–Canada Agreement (USMCA) and EU–Mercosur
               negotiations show how elevated IP standards can constrain
               domestic policy space affecting public health, cultural rights,
               and the region's pursuit of economic sustainability. The analysis
               of institutional design and enforcement practices reveals
               significant disparities between formal commitments to higher IP
               protections and on-the-ground realities of weak enforcement,
               judicial backlogs, and limited administrative capacity. By
               comparing national and regional experiences, the chapter examines
               how tensions intersect with broader goals of sustainable
               development and underlines the institutional gaps that hinder the
               creation of sustainability-oriented IP regimes. Ultimately, it
               argues that fostering economic sustainability under intensified
               IP obligations will require active regional cooperation,
               innovative domestic policies, and careful balancing of competing
               priorities.",
