  volume    =  96,
  number    =  4,
  pages     = "767--777",
  abstract  = "[Court-ordered redistricting in the mid-1960s eradicated severe
               disparities in the populations of U.S. state legislative
               districts. We examine the geographic distribution of money by
               states to counties. Cross-sectional analysis shows that counties
               with relatively more legislative seats per person prior to
               redistricting received relatively more transfers from the state
               per person. Over time, counties that lost legislative seats
               subsequently received a smaller share of state funds per capita.
