  publisher = "Japanese Political Science Association",
  address   = "Tokyo",
  abstract  = "The pandemic in early 2020 has highlighted the role of the
               nation-state as a regulating entity. Governments continue to
               conduct diplomatic activi- ties through public diplomacy. While
               diplomats operate within a bureaucratic system and are subject to
               political control by top leadership in administrative terms, they
               sometimes make bellicose claims that would typically be
               considered undesirable in diplomacy. The Ministry of Foreign
               Affairs has a higher number of designated positions compared to
               other government departments. Based on Weber’s theory of
               bureaucracy, we suggest that such departments may deviate from
               top leadership and implement autonomous policies.We examine how
               diplomats execute foreign policy amidst the tension be- tween
               political control based on principal-agent relationships and
               bureaucratic incentives. Focusing on China, this study identified
               approximately 200 diplo- mats registered on X, collected their
               posts, and gathered personnel data. Using quantitative text
               analysis and regression analysis combined with case studies, we
               found that individual discretion among diplomats is substantial,
               with diplo- mats engaging in aggressive propaganda aimed at their
               own promotion. This result indicates that even within
               authoritarian regimes where political control is presumed to
               function, bureaucratic incentives work more than political
               control based on the principal-agent relationship.",
