               measures proposed by a succession of liberal Presidents. Today
               Presidents aren't necessarily liberal and the House of
               Representatives is not necessarily the graveyard of presidential
               proposals. What happened? Congress evolved. It all began with
               airconditioning. In this entertaining tale of one of our most
               august institutions, Nelson Polsby describes how the Democratic
               majority finally succeeded in overcoming the conservative
               coalition, changing the House. The evolution required among other
               things, the disappearance of Dixiecrats from the House Democratic
               caucus. Dixiecrats were replaced by the rise of the Republican
               party in the south. The Republican party in southern states was
               strengthened by an influx of migrants from the north, who came
               south to settle after the introduction of residential air
               conditioning, which made the climate more tolerable to
               Northerners. This evolutionary process led to the House's
               liberalization and concluded with the House's later
