@TECHREPORT{Yamaguchi_undated-fg,
  title     = "Marginalization of white women in affirmative action debate",
  author    = "Yamaguchi, Haruka",
  publisher = "University of Tokyo",
  abstract  = "Understanding how affirmative action is framed in congressional
               debates-particularly from the perspective of congresswomen-is
               essential for identifying the narratives that shape public and
               legislative interpretations of the policy. Although previous
               research demonstrates that white women have been among the
               primary beneficiaries of affirmative action, it remains unclear
               whether congressional discourse acknowledges this reality. This
               study analyzes congressional records from 1995 to 2025 to examine
               how congresswomen discuss affirmative action and whether they
               incorporate gendered or race-gender-specific frames, including
               references to white women. The findings reveal that congressional
               debates led by women overwhelmingly frame affirmative action in
               racial terms, while references to gender appear less frequently.
               Notably, across all analyzed speeches, congresswomen made no
               explicit mention of white women, suggesting a substantial
               rhetorical omission. These results highlight the persistent
               racialization of affirmative action debates and point to the
               discursive invisibility of white women despite their documented
               gains from such policies.",
