@MISC{Henninger2025-us,
  title        = "Who Do Americans Trust to Manage Elections? Partisan Election
                  Administration and Its Effects on Voter Confidence",
  author       = "Henninger, Phoebe",
  abstract     = "While most democracies rely on independent bodies or
                  nonpartisan civil servants to administer elections, the
                  officials who run elections in the United States are often
                  elected partisans or political appointees. Reformers argue
                  that this structure invites bias and poses a threat to public
                  trust in elections. Despite these concerns, there has been
                  limited empirical research assessing how partisanship is
                  embedded in U.S. election administration systems or how it
                  shapes voter confidence. Using original data on local election
                  structures, national survey data, and a survey experiment, I
                  find that partisan election administration has a latent effect
                  on voter confidence. For most people, election administration
                  is not salient. However, when election problems occur—whether
                  experienced directly or reported secondhand—voters evaluate
                  election administration through a partisan lens. Confidence
                  declines in response to election problems, but those declines
                  are mitigated when the official is a copartisan and
                  exacerbated when the official is from the opposing party.
                  Taken together, the findings suggest that partisan election
                  administration is not inherently damaging to voter confidence.
                  Instead, it becomes consequential when election performance
                  falters. These findings contribute new empirical evidence to
                  debates about election reform and the conditions under which
                  institutional design, political identity, and the voting
                  experience interact to shape trust in elections.",
  year         =  2025,
  howpublished = "\url{https://hdl.handle.net/2027.42/199328}",
  note         = "Accessed: 2026-4-7",
  keywords     = "elections; election administration; political parties;
