@PHDTHESIS{MacPepple2023-zk,
  title     = "The Green generation? Millennials and the future of environmental
               policy. Evidence from the u.{S}. House of representatives",
  author    = "MacPepple, Henrietta Ibiwari",
  publisher = "uh-ir.tdl.org",
  abstract  = "In 2017, there were 6 Millennials in the U.S. House of
               Representatives. By 2021, the number had grown to 32, and 1 in
               the Senate. This recent influx of younger members raises the
               question of whether they prioritize issues that are important to
               younger generations. Studies of representation often find that
               congressmembers from underrepresented groups are more likely to
               advance the interests of their respective groups. The goal of my
               study is to analyze whether Millennials in Congress prioritize
               issues that are important to younger generations. In this
               dissertation, I focus on the environment and climate change as
               public opinion research consistently indicates that Millennials
               and Gen Z are more likely to consider these issues the most
               important problem the U.S. is facing. In my analysis, I compare
               roll-call votes, sponsorships and cosponsorships on environmental
               policies, as well as floor speeches related to the environment
               from 2017 to 2022. I find that Millennial Congressmembers are
               more likely to vote in favor of environmental policies relative
               to older generations, however the effects are mediated by party
               affiliation: Millennial members of the GOP are more likely to
               take a pro-environment position during roll-call votes. In my
               analysis of bill sponsorship activity. I find that being a
               Millennial has no meaningful effect on the degree of attention
               given to environmental policies across both parties Finally,
               through content analysis of one-minute floor speeches, my results
               indicate that there are no generational differences between
               Millennials and older generations in delivering speeches about
               the environment.",
  month     =  may,
  year      =  2023,
  keywords  = "representation, millennials; Thesis",
  language  = "en"
}

