  number    =  1,
  pages     = "53--74",
  abstract  = "[The policy literature often mentions the agenda-setting
               influence of focusing events, but few policy studies
               systematically examine the dynamics of these events. This article
               closes this gap by examining focusing events, group mobilization
               and agenda-setting. Using natural disasters and industrial
               accidents as examples, most focusing events change the dominant
               issues on the agenda in a policy domain, they can lead to
               interest group mobilization, and groups often actively seek to
               expand or contain issues after a focusing event. I explain how
               differences in the composition of policy communities and the
               nature of the events themselves influence group and agenda
