@PHDTHESIS{Essig2025-ea,
  title    = "Opinion Change in American Politics: From Elites to Citizens",
  author   = "Essig, Joseph",
  editor   = "Rothenberg, Lawrence",
  abstract = "This dissertation examines the theme of opinion change in American
              political behavior across two distinct but related areas: first,
              the behavior of legislators and party organizations in Congress,
              and the second: individuals' responses to frames and political
              messages. Chapter 1 traces the effects of party leadership on
              legislators' opinions in the House of representatives from 1955 to
              1987, comparing members' initial opinions concerning bills with
              their final votes. I find strong evidence of party effects on
              voting, and quantify the consequences of using roll call votes to
              capture individual preferences. Roll call estimates of ideology
              overestimate members' natural party loyalty and exaggerate the
              degree to which preferences alone drive voting behavior. Even in
              the less polarized historical period under study, members
              systematically change their positions in patterns consistent with
              party persuasion.Chapter 2 examines the effects of issue frames on
              individual political attitudes, revisiting the canonical
              Expectancy Value (EV) model of emphasis framing to bring empirical
              work closer to theory. Despite scholarly attention to framing
              mechanisms, few studies have directly measured importance weights
              and consideration content which are observable but often go
              unmeasured. To remedy this, I develop a theory of emphasis
              framing, employ a novel within-subject design, and take a new
              approach to measuring importance weights. Experimental results
              illustrate the utility of my approach, showing that there is much
              to be learned about the effects of frames beyond their ATEs, given
              sufficient attention to theory. Chapter 3 examines inequality as
              an overlooked determinant of climate policy preferences in the
              mass public in two survey experimental studies. Our first study
              investigates the effects of factual information on willingness to
              pay for environmental protection and redistribution; the second
              examines self-perception of social status and the framing of the
              distributional consequences of climate change. Results show that
              perceptions of inequality and climate attitudes and behaviors are
              resistant to change, with the exception of subjective willingness
              to pay through taxes. They also highlight the importance of
              beliefs about inequality in conditioning responses to information
              and political messages. Our study underscores the need for
              additional attention to social and economic inequality in
              environmental politics.",
  year     =  2025,
  school   = "University of Rochester",
  language = "en"
}

