@PHDTHESIS{Mochizuki1982-cb,
  title    = "{MANAGING} {AND} {INFLUENCING} {THE} {JAPANESE} {LEGISLATIVE}
              {PROCESS}: {THE} {ROLE} {OF} {PARTIES} {AND} {THE} {NATIONAL}
              {DIET}",
  author   = "Mochizuki, Mike Masato",
  address  = "Ann Arbor, United States",
  abstract = "This study of Japan's legislative process presents the argument
              that the dominance of the Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) along
              with the party's organizational attributes and the National Diet's
              structures and procedures cause (1) the Diet to have significant
              decisional capacity on legislation, (2) the government to place
              major emphasis on the management of both the ruling and opposition
              parties during the legislative process, (3) the ruling party to
              institutionalize norms of inter-party conflict accommodation to
              maintain smooth Diet proceedings and to keep the perennial
              opposition in the parliamentary game, and (4) the opposition
              forces to participate in the policy process as ``pressure
              parties.'' The unanimity norm on procedural questions in the Diet,
              the short deliberative sessions, the committee system, and the
              Diet's bicameral nature contribute to the viscosity of the
              parliamentary process. As illustrated in the case study of the
              revision of the Anti-Monopoly Law, the LDP's decision-making
              process, the nature of intra-party factionalism, and the role of
              veteran politicians aid the party in managing conflict within the
              governmental system. The studies of specific Diet sessions
              demonstrate that the government will suffer severe consequences if
              it pursues a high postured Diet strategy and that the degree of
              government-opposition accommodation depends not only upon the
              political styles of the government-LDP leadership but also upon
              the state of support within the party for the leadership. Survey
              and voting data reveal a high level of inter-party agreement on a
              number of domestic policy issues as well as differences of opinion
              within the LDP. Through case studies of the Small and Medium
              Enterprise Domain Protection Law of 1977, the Employment Insurance
              Law of 1974, and the Yasukuni Shrine Bill, the thesis shows that
              the opposition can influence the legislative process by
              stimulating, altering and blocking legislation. Although
              government-opposition accommodation became more pronounced in the
              1970's, this practice has its roots in the early postwar period
              and the change in the LDP's posture and strategy in the early
              1960's. A ``strategic'' perspective of party systems suggests that
              the character of Japan's legislative process may be intrinsic to
              dominant party systems. The comparison of Japan and Italy appears
              to support this assertion.",
  year     =  1982,
  school   = "Harvard University",
  language = "en"
}

