               of religious conviction. The news media broadcast this message
               with enthusiasm, and white evangelicals rallied to the
               president’s cause. With their support, Reagan won reelection and
               continued to dismantle the welfare state, unraveling a political
               consensus that stood for half a century. In Righting the American
               Dream, Diane Winston reveals how support for Reagan emerged from
               a new religious vision of American identity circulating in the
               popular press. Through four key events—the “evil empire” speech,
               AIDS outbreak, invasion of Grenada, and rise in American poverty
               rates—Winston shows that many journalists uncritically adopted
