@PHDTHESIS{Burkett1997-df,
  title    = "Cosponsorship in the United States Senate: A network analysis of
              Senate communication and leadership, 1973-1990",
  author   = "Burkett, Tracy Lynn",
  editor   = "Skvoretz, John",
  address  = "Ann Arbor, United States",
  abstract = "This study examines ties of cosponsorship in the United States
              Senate. Ties of cosponsorship between Senate enterprises are
              viewed as important communication links. Properties of the
              networks created by these links are analyzed, a model of Senate
              communication is developed, and trends in cosponsorship across
              senates are discussed. Social network theory and methods provide
              the analytical framework for the study. Distributions of
              enterprise characteristics such as seniority, party affiliation,
              and ideology are argued to pattern properties of the cosponsorship
              network including density, reciprocity, and tie strength.
              Transformations to Senate culture since the 1960s, such as
              declines of the norms of seniority and reciprocity, are thought to
              contribute to structural changes in the cosponsorship network.
              Seniority has little effect on this network. Party affiliation and
              ideology influence the distribution and reciprocation of
              cosponsorship ties. Ties sent by minority party enterprises to
              other minority party enterprises have a greater likelihood of
              being reciprocated than ties sent between members of the majority
              party or across party lines. Minority party enterprises have lower
              density of ties and lower tie strengths those of the majority
              party. There is a general decline in reciprocity across the nine
              senates studied.",
  year     =  1997,
