@INCOLLECTION{Brady2012-af,
  title     = "Introduction: Foreign bodies",
  author    = "Brady, A-M and Brown, D",
  booktitle = "Foreigners and Foreign Institutions in Republican China",
  publisher = "Taylor and Francis",
  pages     = "1--22",
  abstract  = "China’s passage through the multipolar, late colonial world of
               the first half of the twentieth century was at once highly
               creative and deeply traumatizing. Though the history of
               Republican China between the First World War and the Chinese
               Communist victory of 1949 is an essential and widely ramifying
               chapter in the wrenching constitution of both modern China and
               the modern world, it is a history that has too often remained
               buried under the accumulated devastation wrought by the Chinese
               civil wars, the Imperial Japanese invasion, and the Second World
               War, or that has too often been distorted through the
               oversimplifying polarizations of half a century of Communist
               Party and Cold Warinfluenced historiography. Regardless of the
               effects of ideology and cataclysmic warfare on historical memory,
               the political and cultural complexities of Republican China in
               and of themselves can make the period difficult to comprehend.
               Having revolted in 1911 from their dynastic past, the Chinese
               were open as never before to the startling and complex
               non-Chinese modern world that was everywhere being subjected to
               profound crises. Within China, no one ideological camp prevailed;
               unsettling foreign influences were rampant and no single Chinese
               or foreign political entity could bring all Chinese territory
               under its control. These unstable conditions, and the
               opportunities that a modernizing China represented to foreigners,
               attracted an uncommon diversity of foreign interests, groups and
               individuals. Missionaries, adventurers, businessmen, diplomats,
               academics, English teachers, humanitarians, refugees, hedonists,
               political operatives, sailors and tourists, from Japan, the
               rivalrous West, the Soviet Union and elsewhere, all found their
               way to China. Many became intimately involved in creating or in
               trying to resolve China’s state of upheaval; all looked around in
               astonishment and consternation. And their presence, national
               interests and conflicting ideologies complicated China’s
               agonizing rebirth into a modern united nation state. As these
               chapters on the foreign experience of Republican China
               demonstrate, foreigners were participant-observers in a
               tumultuous process of civilizational and national transformation,
               and they and their institutions would be almost as profoundly
               altered by what they experienced in China. Through the Republican
               period China was economically, politically and culturally
               diverse, presenting radically different possibilities for
               foreigners and Chinese alike. Thestories in this volume capture
               some of the roles foreigners played in Republican China; its
               analyses suggest some of the ways foreigners and foreign
               institutions helped shape the complex heterogeneous creative
               China of the day; even more clearly, both stories and analyses
               reveal how variously and thoroughly encounters with China changed
               those foreign sojourners. A defining feature of semi-colonial
               China was the existence of enclaves, grounded in the
               extraterritorial legal and concession privileges of foreign
               powers, in which foreigners and Chinese could interact in hybrid
               internationalized spaces, or ‘contact zones’, as Mary Louise
               Pratt has described such places.1 Unsurprisingly, as in earlier
               understandings of Republican China, the diplomatic and social
               problems of extraterritoriality recur as a political theme in our
               contributors’ chapters. Extraterritoriality and the other
               legacies that foreigners in China had inherited from
               nineteenth-century imperialism and the overthrown Qing dynasty
               have long been condemned as outrageous insults to national
               sovereignty and pride, yet these legacies were the enabling
               material condition of the China – creative, provocatively
               heterotopic, even inchoately pluralistic (as Stephen R. MacKinnon
               describes the wartime capital of Wuhan in 19382) – that stirs in
               the background of the foreign experiences studied in this
               collection. Our wide-ranging chapters demonstrate just how
               complex the physical and imaginary spaces which foreigners helped
               create had become in the Republican period. Such interactive
               spaces permitted the Chinese to nurture new and other Chinas, and
               to engage in reforming and modernizing economic, cultural,
               ideological, or political work. These enclaves of alterity,
               rather than simply oppressing the Chinese people, as a strictly
               liberatory ahistorical anti-colonial narrative of China’s modern
               past would have it, could also provide an ambiguous haven for
               Chinese citizens, including the founders of the Chinese Communist
               Party and the Kuomintang, in which they could experiment with new
               Chinese identities. Rudolph Wagner notes that innovation in
               modern Chinese society ‘hinged on the fact that somewhere on
               Chinese soil alternative options were present which could not be
               removed by a government which was bound to feel pressured to
               reestablish’ an imperious, sovereign Chinese homogeneity.3 Wagner
               extends this observation on the critical roles the foreign
               enclaves played in allowing the Chinese to develop a dynamic
               modern public sphere to the very fact of the presence, even
               outside extraterritorial enclaves, of foreigners as ‘walking
               niches’ of alternative modernities.4 Suggestions of the
               stimulating roles played by foreigners, and the Chinese spaces
               they had claimed, are evident throughout the chapters in this
               collection. As a young English language student (cited by
               contributor Eric Henry) supposed in 1920s Peking, foreigners and
               foreign things in China should be seen both as irritants harmful
               to the Chinese body politic and as stimuli provoking a Chinese
               response which could sometimes produce pearls. This ambivalent
               estimation needs to be kept in mind since the irritating issues
               of extraterritoriality and of dominating foreign presences recur
               and provide – as do violence and warfare – a unifying political
               thread running across the Chinese and Sino-foreign landscapes
               explored in this volume and through the successive Chinese and
               international crises of the three decades it covers. © 2013
               Anne-Marie Brady and Douglas Brown.",
  year      =  2012
}

